.TH CKSUM 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
cksum \- print CRC checksums and sizes of files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cksum
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cksum
prints a 32-bit CRC and the size in bytes of each
.IR file .
With no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input.
.PP
The CRC is the one specified by POSIX for
.BR cksum :
polynomial 0x04C11DB7, most significant bit first, starting from zero.
It covers the contents followed by their length in bytes, least
significant byte first, in as few bytes as the length needs, so none for
an empty file.
The result is complemented.
It is not the CRC-32 of zip, gzip, and PNG, which
.BR crc32 (1)
prints.
.PP
Each output line is the CRC in decimal, a space, the size in bytes, a
space, and the file name as given on the command line.
When standard input is read because there are no operands, the name and
the space before it are left out.
For an operand of
.BR \- ,
the name is
.BR \- .
.PP
A file that cannot be opened is reported on standard error and skipped,
and the remaining files are still read.
An error while reading a file ends the command at once.
.SH OPTIONS
.B cksum
takes no options other than
.BR \-\-help .
Any other argument that begins with a dash, except
.B \-
itself, is an invalid option, even after a
.IR file ,
unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
An invalid option prints a usage message on standard error.
.B \-\-
ends the options, for a
.I file
whose name begins with a dash.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded when the program starts, even in
quoted operands, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A pattern that matches nothing is left as it is.
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
names written to a console are not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every file was read, or
.B \-\-help
was given.
.TP
.B 1
A file could not be opened or read, an option was invalid, or standard
output could not be written.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe whose reader exited before all output was
written.
If the pipe breaks only when the output still buffered at exit is
written, the status is 1 instead.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the CRC and size of standard input:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf abc | cksum
1219131554 3
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the CRC and size of a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cksum abc.txt
1219131554 3 abc.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
List DLLs in two directories by checksum, so that copies with the same
contents are adjacent:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cksum old/*.dll new/*.dll | sort
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU coreutils:
.IP \(bu 2
There are no options besides
.BR \-\-help .
GNU
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-\-tag ,
and the rest have no equivalent.
Use
.B md5sum
and the related commands for other algorithms and for checking lists.
.IP \(bu 2
The output is the same as that of GNU
.B cksum
without options.
.PP
The usage message shows
.I file
as required, but it is optional.
.PP
Standard input is closed after it is read, so a second
.B \-
operand fails with
.BR "Bad file descriptor" ,
and the command ends there.
.PP
Files and standard input are read as binary, so checksums match those
of the same bytes on other systems, and a text file with CR LF line
endings does not match a copy with LF line endings.
Output lines end with LF.
.PP
A
.I file
may be a Windows path, with a drive letter and either kind of slash, or
a busybox-w32 device name such as
.IR /dev/null .
It is printed exactly as given.
On Windows 10 version 1903 or later, file names may contain any Unicode
characters, and on older systems only those of the system code page.
A directory operand fails with
.BR "Permission denied" .
.PP
When standard output is a console, names are converted for display from
the ANSI code page to the console's output code page.
.PP
A 32-bit CRC detects accidental damage, not deliberate tampering.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR crc32 (1),
.BR md5sum (1),
.BR sum (1)
